# Pinned tool binaries are FETCHED, never committed — strape/scripts/fetch-tool.mjs and fetch-osv.mjs
# download them and verify the sha256 against the digest GitHub publishes for the release asset.
#
# The `.sha256` files ARE tracked: they are the pin, and they are what `fetch-tool.mjs --verify` checks an
# installed binary against. Committing the binaries themselves would put ~50MB of unreviewable machine code
# in a repo whose entire premise is that you can read what you ship.
#
# Deliberately HERE rather than as more lines in the root .gitignore, which already names osv-scanner, syft
# and cosign individually. That file is inside the review-attestation digest (`review-attest.mjs`
# SCOPE_FILES) because hunk 6 lives in it, so every new tool would cost a re-sign — a human signature spent
# on a build artefact. A directory-local rule costs nothing and covers tools nobody has added yet.
*
!.gitignore
!*.sha256
